cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "Please cite the relevant theorem paper and computational specification, and cite the underlying arithmetic sources separately."
type: software
title: "Polynomial presentations of the integers over the rationals"
authors:
  - name: "JUVa"
repository-code: "https://github.com/JoseBrox/diophantine-z-q_improvements"
url: "https://github.com/JoseBrox/diophantine-z-q_improvements"
date-released: 2026-11-10
version: "2026.11.10-v22"
license: CC-BY-4.0
abstract: >-
  Mathematical papers and exact computational specifications for polynomial
  presentations of the integers over the rationals under stated arithmetic
  hypotheses. The leading existence constructions have 25 variables with
  degree less than 2^85 and 226 variables with exact degree four, with
  ineffective sufficient coefficient thresholds. A fully specified polynomial
  has 27 variables, degree less than 2^87, and coefficient height less than
  2^(2^99), using fixed exponent 20483. A fully specified quartic has
  262 variables and coefficient height 149^38730+1, with 279599 binary digits.
  A thirteen-variable family under weak abc with exponent 121/100,
  implied by ordinary Szpiro, has degree at most 11774654098433 and
  support less than 2^508. A second thirteen-variable family under weak
  abc with exponent 16/15 has degree at most 11212784195585 and support
  less than 2^507. Ordinary rational abc implies both hypotheses and
  also supplies an eighty-seven-variable quartic. These three conditional
  families have ineffective sufficient input shifts. The exact quartic
  monomial counts are 2835, 3494, and 918. The unexpanded 25- and
  27-variable determinant polynomials have certified support bounds
  less than 2^1979 and 2^2192, respectively.
  Structural results include guarded finite-algebra elimination, modular
  multiplicity and contact-height estimates, elliptic height estimates,
  optimal square predicates, number-field definability reductions, and
  local obstructions to specified constructions. Every variable count
  includes the input. An unconditional local theorem proves that a single
  rational function of the input and slope cannot uniformly detect both
  odd contact valuations on the prescribed eighth-power scale. Further results
  determine the genus of the quadratic contact covers, obstruct finite
  algebraic reconstruction of products of elliptic indices, and prove
  uniform input-height inequalities with an explicit four-contact
  counterexample to a logarithmic odd-radical bound.
keywords:
  - Diophantine definitions
  - Hilbert's tenth problem
  - rational points
  - explicit polynomials
  - elliptic heights
  - finite algebraic elimination
  - number fields
